# No Legacy, No Dead Code

NinjaCode has not shipped. There are **no external consumers** of any package, so there is **no backward compatibility to preserve**. Every internal API can be renamed, moved, or deleted freely. This is a standing licence to keep the codebase clean — use it.

## Never leave legacy in advance

A change ships **one** path. Do not leave the old path beside the new one "just in case", add a migration adapter for an internal API that has no external consumers, or mark `@deprecated` without deleting in the same change.

Forbidden for internal product code:

- One-shot migrators (`migrateXIfNeeded`, `legacyYToZ`) when callers can be updated instead
- Dual response / dual setting shims (`a ?? b`, fallback to a removed key)
- Keeping both `/v1/old` and `/v1/new` while only in-repo clients exist — migrate callers, delete the old route

External disk conventions users may already have (e.g. `.ninjacode/rules`, `.mcp.json`) are not "legacy shims"; product code may keep reading them.

## A refactor replaces, it never doubles

Move the code, update every caller, delete the old location — all in the same commit.

- No compatibility re-exports, no deprecated aliases, no wrapper that forwards to the new name.
- No two implementations of the same thing coexisting because the old one might still be useful. Pick one, delete the other.
- No `legacy` branches, no `v2` suffixes alongside a `v1`, no dead feature flags.
- No `@deprecated` without deleting in the same change — if it can go, it goes now.

```typescript
// BAD — a transition that will never end
export { softenSupersededReads as dropSupersededReads } from "./context.js";

// GOOD — rename the symbol, fix the callers, done
export { softenSupersededReads } from "./context.js";
```

## Dead code is deleted, not archived

- Any export nothing consumes is deleted. `pnpm knip` reports these; the answer is never an entry in the ignore list.
- No code kept "for later". A future need will be written better with that era's context. Git history is the safety net — keeping a dead symbol in `main` is not a backup, it is a maintenance cost.
- No commented-out code blocks.

## Comments do not describe the past

A comment explaining what the code used to do, why it changed, or that a call is "now" handled elsewhere is noise the moment the PR merges. That belongs in the commit message.

```typescript
// BAD
// We used to spawn git directly here, now goes through ProcessRunner
// (kept the old path in mind for the checkpoint case)

// GOOD — nothing. The code shows what it does; git shows how it got there.
```

## Enforcement

`pnpm knip` (unused files, exports, dependencies) and `pnpm depcruise` (orphan modules, cycles) run in CI. When either flags something, delete the code — do not widen the config.
